  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe active pattern continues Tuesday through Wednesday night, as \nupper ridging begins to amplify across the western CONUS.\n\nTuesday afternoon, surface low pressure will deepen over CO, as a \ncold front sags south from north central NE. South of the cold \nfront, moderate to strong instability is expected to develop by \nafternoon. Forecast soundings show steep midlevel lapse rates, with \nvertically veering wind profiles supporting 40+ kt effective shear. \nInitial supercells could pose a risk for large hail and severe \ngusts. The area of greater concern will be southeast of of a line \nfrom Arthur through O'Neill, where SPC is forecasting a Slight Risk \nof severe storms. With time, sufficient clustering could occur ahead \nof the front as a low-level jet increases during the evening, and \nMCS development may occur across central into southeast NE. Likely \nPOPS Tuesday afternoon and Tuesday night, especially south of \nHighway 2 and 91.\n\nWednesday, models have been indicating a fairly strong disturbance \ndropping southeast through the region during the afternoon and \nevening. Storms may linger into Wednesday morning from Tuesday night \nactivity as well. Storms developing during the afternoon and evening \nmay be strong to severe, as 0-6km shear ranges from 45-50 kts. With \nhighs only in the mid to upper 70s, instability may be limited to \naround 1500 J/KG. POPs are fairly high at 50 to 60 percent Wednesday \ninto Wednesday evening. \n\nUpper ridging over the Rockies will gradually build eastward into \nthe plains by this weekend, with drier conditions expected Thursday \nthrough Sunday. Highs near to slightly below normal in the upper 70s \nto mid 80s Thursday, then slightly above normal in the 80s, with \nnear 90 southwest Friday through Sunday.",
